      "text": "To solve the given system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n3x - 5y &= -11, \\quad \\text{(1)}\\\\\n7x + 2y &= -12. \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\nwe will use the method of elimination. The goal is to eliminate one of the variables by adding or subtracting the equations.\n\nFirst, we will try to eliminate \\( y \\). To do this, we need the coefficients of \\( y \\) in both equations to be opposites. Let's multiply equation (1) by 2 and equation (2) by 5 to make the coefficients of \\( y \\) equal in magnitude but opposite in sign.\n\nMultiply equation (1) by 2:\n\\[\n2(3x - 5y) = 2(-11) \\implies 6x - 10y = -22. \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\nMultiply equation (2) by 5:\n\\[\n5(7x + 2y) = 5(-12) \\implies 35x + 10y = -60. \\quad \\text{(4)}\n\\]\n\nNow, add equations (3) and (4) to eliminate \\( y \\):\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60) \\implies 41x = -82.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-82}{41} = -2.\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( x = -2 \\) back into one of the original equations to solve for \\( y \\). We will use equation (1):\n\\[\n3(-2) - 5y = -11 \\implies -6 - 5y = -11.\n\\]\n\nAdd 6 to both sides:\n\\[\n-5y = -11 + 6 \\implies -5y = -5.\n\\]\n\nDivide by -5:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{-5}{-5} = 1.\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 1)}.\n\\]\n\nTo verify, substitute \\( x = -2 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\) back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 3(-2) - 5(1) = -6 - 5 = -11 \\) (True)\n2. \\( 7(-2) + 2(1) = -14 + 2 = -12 \\) (True)\n\nThe solution is correct. The ordered pair \\((x, y)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(-2, 1)}\\).",
