      "text": "We want to find the values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy both equations. To do this, we can start by multiplying the first equation by two and the second equation by three, allowing us to eliminate one of the variables. \n\n\\begin{align*}\n6x-10y&=-22,\\\\\n21x+6y&=-36.\n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, we can multiply the first equation by three and the second equation by two to eliminate the $y$ terms:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n18x-30y&=-66,\\\\\n42x+12y&=-72.\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow, we can add these two equations together to eliminate the $y$ terms:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(18x-30y)+(42x+12y)&=-66+(-72),\\\\\n60x-18y&=-138.\n\\end{align*}\n\nThe combined equation $(60x-18y)=-138$ reduces to $10x-3y=-23$. To make the coefficients of $y$ sum to zero, we can multiply the equation by two:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n20x-6y&=-46.\n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, we'll add this new equation to the original first equation to get rid of the $x$ terms:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(3x-5y)+(10x-6y)&=-11+(-46),\\\\\n13x-11y&=-57.\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow we have the one-variable equation $13x-11y=-57$. We also have the equation $10x-3y=-23$. To eliminate the $x$ term, we will multiply the first equation by 10 and the second equation by 13:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n130x-55y&=-570,\\\\\n130x-39y&=-299.\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow, subtract the second equation from the first equation. This eliminates the $x$ terms:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(130x-55y)-(130x-39y)&=-570-(-299),\\\\\n-16y&=-271.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSolving for $y$ gives us:\n\n\\begin{align*}\ny&=\\frac{-271}{-16},\\\\\ny&=\\frac{271}{16}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow, we can substitute this value of $y$ into the equation $10x-3y=-23$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n10x-3\\left(\\frac{271}{16}\\right)&=-23,\\\\\n10x-\\frac{813}{16}&=-23,\\\\\n10x&=-23+\\frac{813}{16},\\\\\n10x&=\\frac{-26-813}{16},\\\\\n10x&=\\frac{-839}{16},\\\\\nx&=\\frac{-839}{160}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nThe solution to the system of equations is $\\left(\\frac{-839}{160},\\frac{271}{16}\\right)$.\nThe system of equations can be rewritten as\n\\begin{align*}\n3x-5y &= -11,\\\\\n7x+2y &= -12.\n\\end{align*}\nWe can solve this system by multiplying the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 5, giving us\n\\begin{align*}\n6x-10y &= -22,\\\\\n35x+10y &= -60.\n\\end{align*}\nAdding these equations together, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n41x &= -82,\\\\\nx &= \\frac{-82}{41}.\n\\end{align*}\nSubstituting this value of $x$ into the first equation, we can solve for $y$:\n\\begin{align*}\n3\\left(\\frac{-82}{41}\\right) - 5y &= -11,\\\\\n-\\frac{246}{41} - 5y &= -11,\\\\\n-5y &= \\frac{1}{41},\\\\\ny &= \\frac{-1}{205}.\n\\end{align*}\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $\\boxed{\\left(\\frac{-82}{41}, \\frac{-1}{205}\\right)}$.The answer is: (-\\frac{82}{41},-\\frac{1}{205})",
