      "text": "To solve the given system of equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -11, \\\\\n7x + 2y = -12,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nwe can use the method of elimination. The goal is to eliminate one of the variables by making the coefficients of that variable in both equations equal (or opposite).\n\nFirst, let's label the equations for reference:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -11 \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)} \\\\\n7x + 2y = -12 \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate \\(y\\), we need the coefficients of \\(y\\) to be equal in magnitude but opposite in sign. We can achieve this by finding a common multiple of \\(-5\\) and \\(2\\). The least common multiple of \\(5\\) and \\(2\\) is \\(10\\). Therefore, we will multiply Equation 1 by \\(2\\) and Equation 2 by \\(5\\) to make the coefficients of \\(y\\) equal in magnitude but opposite in sign:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot (3x - 5y) = 2 \\cdot (-11) \\implies 6x - 10y = -22,\n\\]\n\\[\n5 \\cdot (7x + 2y) = 5 \\cdot (-12) \\implies 35x + 10y = -60.\n\\]\n\nNow we have the modified system:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n6x - 10y = -22, \\\\\n35x + 10y = -60.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nNext, we add these two equations together to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60) \\implies 41x = -82.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-82}{41} = -2.\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(x = -2\\), we substitute this value back into one of the original equations to solve for \\(y\\). Let's use Equation 1:\n\n\\[\n3x - 5y = -11 \\implies 3(-2) - 5y = -11 \\implies -6 - 5y = -11.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n-6 - 5y = -11 \\implies -5y = -11 + 6 \\implies -5y = -5 \\implies y = 1.\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 1)}\n\\]",
